  "tldr": "Capacity-constrained treatment choice at a beyond-sample loss quantile is governed by the inverse of a selected heterogeneous survival mixture, not by separate cellwise extreme effects. The project derives the exact minimax expected logarithmic-regret frontier over an atom-permitting Hall class, including the sparse-dominance and collective-contribution phases, with a computable thresholded sorting-and-inversion learner and a same-class all-measurable lower bound. A separate inference rung constructs honest simultaneous intervals for every feasible policy value and asks for the exact regime in which their maximal width vanishes, so selection validity is obtained without presuming that confidence width and regret share a rate.",
  "project_justification": {
    "gap": "Published quantile-policy methods study fixed levels or bounded outcomes, regular policy-learning theory studies additive mean welfare, and heterogeneous extreme-value theory studies fixed contrasts or predetermined mixtures. None controls expected log loss after a data-dependent capacity allocation changes which Hall components enter a population mixture before extreme-quantile inversion.",
    "niche": "The discrete randomized design makes identification exact and makes the capacity optimizer computable by sorting, while heterogeneous local tail-index ties and random empty cells retain a nonregular statistical problem with a concrete product-prior bottleneck.",
    "fill": "The analysis combines Hall splices, exact soft mixture loss, and capacity exchange structure to characterize regret across all joint sequences, then propagates bias-aware cell-tail regions through the same sorting and generalized inversion to obtain honest post-selection inference."
  },
  "related_work": "OsamaZachariahStoica2020 provides the matching safety-critical upper-cost-quantile objective and finite-sample conformal motivation, but its support-based limit does not extrapolate an unbounded rare-loss tail. WangZhouSongSherwood2018 and XiaZhangKong2026 study marginal quantile-optimal treatment regimes at fixed quantile levels. KitagawaTetenov2018 and AtheyWager2021 obtain constrained-policy regret guarantees for regular additive mean welfare, where empirical-process deviations enter before a linear objective rather than before nonlinear tail-mixture inversion. CarpentierKim2015 and BoucheronThomas2015 provide the Hall testing modulus and threshold-selection ingredients for one tail. EinmahlHe2023 makes the average distribution the relevant heterogeneous tail object, PanovMorozova2021 exhibits sparse-impurity phase changes, and DaouiaPadoanStupfler2024 develops pooling under common-tail structure; none allows a policy to select the mixture components. SasakiWang2024 supplies the worst-case-bias principle for honest scalar tail inference, HuangLiPeng2025 supplies process inference for fixed treatment contrasts, and AndrewsKitagawaMcCloskey2024 supplies the regular winner-inference comparison. GuggenbergerMehtaPavlov2026 shows why unrestricted bounded fixed-quantile treatment choice can be minimax-uninformative, while the Hall envelope and moving beyond-sample quantile here produce a distinct rate problem. WangCaiWangShiTang2026 estimates conditional extreme treatment effects under a linear common-index structure and therefore does not subsume arbitrary heterogeneous population-mixture policy optimization.",
  "technical_internal_limitation": "The baseline Assouad argument controls expected missed-cell count, but the exact logarithmic loss is a concave log-sum-exp function of that count. Equal expected mistake counts can yield different expected regrets when extrapolation is deep. A valid full lower bound must control the distribution of ambiguous or missed heavy cells after unrestricted capacity reallocation; Jensen's inequality in the wrong direction does not close this step.",
  "honest_scope": "The population sorting identity, its \\(m=2\\) normalization reduction, finite-policy generalized inversion, Hall-core inversion, two-group dominance equation, generic regret sandwich, two-cell rate, and the displayed simultaneous confidence system are theorem claims to be established. The exact many-cell regret frontier and the sharp honest-width frontier remain open-ended questions. No common tail index, fixed gap, density, continuous outcome law, nonempty cell sample, nuisance-estimator rate, or nonzero policy-selection penalty is assumed. The \\(m=2\\) theorem recovers the translated Hall exponent multiplied by extrapolation depth and supplies an if-and-only-if vanishing criterion.",
